<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Frederick William IV of Prussia</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1795">1795</a>–<a href="/years/1861">1861</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-hohenzollern">House of Hohenzollern</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Frederick William IV was king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> from 1840 to 1861. A romantic conservative who admired the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a>, he was forced by the revolution of 1848 to grant a constitution, but in 1849 refused the imperial crown offered by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankfurt-parliament">Frankfurt Parliament</a>. His plan for a union of German states under Prussia was abandoned at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/olomouc">Olmütz</a> in 1850. After strokes, his brother William ruled as regent from 1858.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/frederick-william-iv-of-prussia.jpg" alt="Frederick William IV of Prussia" width="320" height="417" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Hermann Biow · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Friedrich_Wilhelm_IV_of_Prussia_(1847).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Frederick William was born on 15 October 1795, the son of King Frederick William III of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> and Queen Louise, and was his mother's favourite son. His tutors included the historian and statesman Friedrich Ancillon. When Louise died in 1810 he saw her death as a punishment from God, and he later tried to live in a way that would absolve him of his guilt. The execution of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xvi">Louis XVI</a> in 1793 had helped create the conditions for his later orientation toward tradition and historical continuity. After Prussia lost the Battle of Jena-Auerstedt on 14 October 1806 he and his brother William were taken to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/konigsberg">Königsberg</a> and then fled with their parents to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/klaipeda">Memel</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>He became king in 1840 and was also sovereign prince of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neuchatel">Neuchâtel</a> until 1848. Called the romanticist on the throne, he was deeply religious and believed he ruled by divine right. His ideal was a state governed by the Christian estates rather than a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constitutional-monarchy">constitutional monarchy</a>. He reconciled with the Catholic population by authorising the Cologne Cathedral Building Association in 1840, and in 1842 he founded the civil class of the Order Pour le Mérite on the advice of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-von-humboldt">Alexander von Humboldt</a>. He also promoted architecture, including the Neues Museum in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a>.</p><p>The overthrow of the French July Monarchy in February 1848 set off revolution across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>. About 300 demonstrators were killed in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a> barricade fighting of March, and the king blamed foreigners in his manifesto To my dear Berliners. In December 1848 he dissolved the Prussian National Assembly and imposed a constitution. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankfurt-parliament">Frankfurt Parliament</a> elected him Emperor of the Germans on 28 March 1849. He refused, writing that he did not want it because <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> would then be separated from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>. He sent troops to put down the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dresden">Dresden</a> uprising in May 1849, and his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/erfurt-union">Erfurt Union</a> lost the support of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanover">Hanover</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony">Saxony</a> by the winter of 1849.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>His strokes began on 14 July 1857 and affected his speech. On 7 October 1858 he signed a regency charter for his brother William, and the new regent dismissed the reactionary minister president Otto Theodor von Manteuffel, starting what is called the New Era. Frederick William suffered further strokes in 1859 and 1860 and died on 2 January 1861. He was buried in the Friedenskirche in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/potsdam">Potsdam</a>, and his heart was buried separately beside his parents in the Charlottenburg mausoleum. The Berlin-Potsdam palace landscape and Museum Island, which he extended, and the Cologne Cathedral, which he helped to complete, are now <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Sites</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Frederick William IV” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After the revolutions of 1848–1849, the two princes ceded their principalities to their kinsman, King <b>Frederick William IV of Prussia</b>, and the territories were transferred in 1850, forming the Prussian Hohenzollern Lands.</blockquote><a href="/empire/county-of-hohenzollern">County of Hohenzollern · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1849 <b>Frederick William IV of Prussia</b> refused the imperial crown offered by the Frankfurt Parliament but tried to unite northern Germany in the Erfurt Union, a plan he abandoned under Austrian and Russian pressure in the Punctation of Olmütz in November 1850.</blockquote><a href="/empire/german-confederation">German Confederation · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>The revolution of 1848 led <b>Frederick William IV</b> to grant a constitution, revised in 1850, which set up a parliament, the Landtag, whose lower house was elected by a three-class franchise weighted by taxes paid.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-prussia">Kingdom of Prussia · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>By the 1840s it was well known in Lower Saxony and reportedly a favourite of <b>Frederick William IV of Prussia</b>.</blockquote><a href="/songs/silent-night">Silent Night</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Frederick William IV</b> rebuilt the ancestral castle in neo-Gothic style from 1850 to 1867.</blockquote><a href="/state/principality-of-hohenzollern-hechingen">Principality of Hohenzollern-Hechingen</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/potsdam">Potsdam</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=52.52,13.38&amp;year=1837" aria-label="Berlin on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4850 352 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Frederick William IV of Prussia"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5300 622h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5300 622h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=52.52,13.38&amp;year=1837">What was here in 1837? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
